\section{Report Generation}

The module produces three \LaTeX{} artefacts from a review session, plus a
CSV dump: a \emph{review report} (provenance and the per-paper decision
record), an \emph{audit table} (one row per paper with the reason it was
kept or excluded, meant for a paper's backing appendix), and---through
[[llm_review]]---the literature synthesis, which reuses the decision record
as its appendix. Each of them can be written either as a compilable
standalone document or as a fragment for [[\input]] into a larger document.

The section is organised bottom-up. Escaping comes first, because every
piece of text that reaches \LaTeX{} passes through it. Then the heading
helper and the two document skeletons (standalone and fragment), which are
shared by all artefacts. The report-specific helpers follow: citation
keys, BibTeX entries, the summary tables, and the two body builders
(provenance and decision record) that the report and the synthesis
compose in different ways. The report assembly and the audit table close
the section. When a session has multiple query-provider pairs (from using
different queries with different providers), the report shows them as a
table mapping providers to their respective queries, rather than a single
query field.

\subsection{Escaping for BibTeX and \LaTeX}

Titles, tags and queries come from external metadata and from users, so
they contain characters that \LaTeX{} treats as syntax, and increasingly
characters that pdf\LaTeX{} cannot typeset at all: curly quotes, dashes,
ellipses, and symbols such as $\star$ that publishers put into titles.
The latter used to abort compilation with \enquote{Unicode character not
set up for use with LaTeX}. Rather than asking users to patch the preamble
with [[\DeclareUnicodeCharacter]] for every new symbol, we normalise text
before it is written.

Both escapers make a single pass over the characters. A character is
either a \LaTeX{} special (escaped), a piece of Unicode punctuation with a
\TeX{} equivalent (mapped through [[LATEX_UNICODE_PUNCTUATION]]), a symbol
beyond U+2100 (dropped, since the Latin and accented letters that
[[inputenc]] handles all lie below that point), or ordinary text. A single
pass matters: the naive alternative of mapping punctuation first and then
escaping specials would re-escape the backslash and braces of the
[[\dots{}]] the mapping just inserted.

The BibTeX escaper is deliberately narrower than the \LaTeX{} one: BibTeX
fields are braced already, and braces inside them carry meaning (they
protect capitalisation), so we leave braces and backslashes alone there.

<<review report generation>>=
LATEX_UNICODE_PUNCTUATION: dict[str, str] = {
    "\u201c": "``",          # left double quotation mark
    "\u201d": "''",          # right double quotation mark
    "\u2018": "`",           # left single quotation mark
    "\u2019": "'",           # right single quotation mark
    "\u02bc": "'",           # modifier letter apostrophe
    "\u2032": "'",           # prime
    "\u2013": "--",          # en dash
    "\u2014": "---",         # em dash
    "\u2026": r"\dots{}",    # horizontal ellipsis
    "\u00a0": " ",           # no-break space
    "\u2009": " ",           # thin space
    "\u202f": " ",           # narrow no-break space
    "\u200b": "",            # zero width space
    "\u00ad": "",            # soft hyphen
    "\ufeff": "",            # byte order mark
}

LATEX_SYMBOL_CUTOFF = 0x2100

LATEX_SPECIALS: dict[str, str] = {
    "\\": r"\textbackslash{}",
    "&": r"\&",
    "%": r"\%",
    "$": r"\$",
    "#": r"\#",
    "_": r"\_",
    "{": r"\{",
    "}": r"\}",
    "~": r"\textasciitilde{}",
    "^": r"\textasciicircum{}",
}

BIBTEX_SPECIALS: dict[str, str] = {
    "&": r"\&",
    "%": r"\%",
    "$": r"\$",
    "#": r"\#",
    "_": r"\_",
}


def _escape_chars(text: str, specials: dict[str, str]) -> str:
    """Escape `text` for TeX in one pass using the `specials` map."""
    out: list[str] = []
    for char in text:
        if char in specials:
            out.append(specials[char])
        elif char in LATEX_UNICODE_PUNCTUATION:
            out.append(LATEX_UNICODE_PUNCTUATION[char])
        elif ord(char) > LATEX_SYMBOL_CUTOFF:
            continue
        else:
            out.append(char)
    return "".join(out)


def escape_bibtex(text: str) -> str:
    """Escape special BibTeX characters and normalise Unicode punctuation."""
    return _escape_chars(text, BIBTEX_SPECIALS)


def escape_latex(text: str) -> str:
    """Escape special LaTeX characters and normalise Unicode punctuation."""
    return _escape_chars(text, LATEX_SPECIALS)
@

Let's verify the single pass: the backslash and braces of an inserted
[[\dots{}]] must survive, and a symbol beyond the cutoff must vanish
without taking its neighbours with it.

<<test functions>>=
class TestLatexEscaping:
    """Tests for the LaTeX and BibTeX escapers."""

    def test_escape_latex_specials(self):
        assert escape_latex("a & b_c 100%") == r"a \& b\_c 100\%"

    def test_escape_latex_backslash_single_pass(self):
        assert escape_latex("a\\b") == r"a\textbackslash{}b"

    def test_escape_latex_maps_unicode_punctuation(self):
        text = "“quoted” – dash…"
        assert escape_latex(text) == "``quoted'' -- dash\\dots{}"

    def test_escape_latex_drops_exotic_symbols(self):
        result = escape_latex("star \u2606 here")
        assert "\u2606" not in result
        assert result == "star  here"

    def test_escape_bibtex_drops_exotic_symbols(self):
        result = escape_bibtex("A \u2606 B & C")
        assert result == r"A  B \& C"

    def test_escape_bibtex_keeps_braces(self):
        assert escape_bibtex("{DNA} & RNA") == r"{DNA} \& RNA"
