\subsection{Markdown and heading levels}

The research context is written by the user in Markdown, so it goes
through pandoc when available; the fallback is escaped plain text, which
loses formatting but never breaks the build.

<<review report generation>>=
def markdown_to_latex(markdown_text: str) -> str:
    """
    Convert Markdown text to LaTeX using pypandoc.

    Falls back to escaped plain text if pypandoc is not available.
    """
    try:
        import pypandoc
        return pypandoc.convert_text(markdown_text, 'latex', format='markdown')
    except Exception:
        return escape_latex(markdown_text)
@

A section builder that emits a heading and a nested sub-heading cannot
hard-code either level: the same builder serves the standalone report,
where it sits at [[\section]] level, and the synthesis, which places the
same content one level down. The Markdown builders already derive the
nested level with [[heading_level + 1]]; [[sub_heading]] is the \LaTeX{}
counterpart. Unknown commands pass through unchanged so a caller can hand
in a custom sectioning command without breaking anything.

<<review report generation>>=
LATEX_SUB_HEADINGS: dict[str, str] = {
    r"\section": r"\subsection",
    r"\subsection": r"\subsubsection",
    r"\subsubsection": r"\paragraph",
}


def sub_heading(heading_command: str) -> str:
    """Return the sectioning command one level below `heading_command`."""
    return LATEX_SUB_HEADINGS.get(heading_command, heading_command)
@

The mapping and the passthrough are quick to pin down:

<<test functions>>=
class TestSubHeading:
    def test_mappings(self):
        assert sub_heading(r"\section") == r"\subsection"
        assert sub_heading(r"\subsection") == r"\subsubsection"
        assert sub_heading(r"\subsubsection") == r"\paragraph"

    def test_passthrough(self):
        assert sub_heading(r"\chapter") == r"\chapter"
