\subsection{Standalone documents and fragments}

Both the report and the synthesis used to carry their own copy of the
preamble, and the copies had drifted: the report loads [[amssymb]] and
[[graphicx]] for its matrix tables, the synthesis did not, so pasting the
report's tables into a synthesis would have failed on [[\checkmark]] and
[[\rotatebox]]. One package list, the union of both, removes that class
of error. [[enumitem]] is loaded although nothing here uses it, to keep
existing output unchanged.

<<review report generation>>=
LATEX_PACKAGES: list[str] = [
    r"\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}",
    r"\usepackage[colorlinks=true,allcolors=blue]{hyperref}",
    r"\usepackage{enumitem}",
    r"\usepackage{longtable}",
    r"\usepackage{booktabs}",
    r"\usepackage{array}",
    r"\usepackage{amssymb}",
    r"\usepackage{graphicx}",
    r"\usepackage[backend=biber,style=authoryear]{biblatex}",
]
@

A standalone document wraps a body in the skeleton a reader expects from a
report: title page, abstract, table of contents, the body, an optional
bibliography command and an optional appendix. The title is escaped here
because session names are free text (an ampersand in a session name used
to be a hard \LaTeX{} error). The bibliography command is passed as a
literal string rather than a flag so a caller can choose its options; the
review report passes none, since it cites everything with [[\fullcite]]
and has no reference list of its own.

<<review report generation>>=
def latex_document(
    body: list[str],
    *,
    title: str,
    date: str,
    bib_resource: str,
    abstract: str | None = None,
    bibliography: str | None = None,
    appendix: list[str] | None = None,
) -> list[str]:
    """Wrap `body` in a compilable LaTeX document."""
    lines: list[str] = [r"\documentclass{article}"]
    lines.extend(LATEX_PACKAGES)
    lines.extend(
        [
            f"\\addbibresource{{{bib_resource}}}",
            "",
            f"\\title{{{escape_latex(title)}}}",
            f"\\date{{{date}}}",
            "",
            r"\begin{document}",
            r"\maketitle",
            "",
        ]
    )
    if abstract is not None:
        lines.extend([r"\begin{abstract}", abstract, r"\end{abstract}"])
    lines.extend(
        [r"\clearpage", r"\clearpage", r"\tableofcontents", r"\clearpage", ""]
    )
    lines.extend(body)
    if bibliography:
        lines.extend([bibliography, ""])
    if appendix:
        lines.append(r"\appendix")
        lines.extend(appendix)
    lines.append(r"\end{document}")
    return lines
@

A fragment is the same body without the skeleton, meant to be read by
[[\input]] from another document's body. It cannot set anything up for
itself: biblatex refuses [[\addbibresource]] outside the preamble, and
package loading is the including document's business. So instead of
declaring, the fragment \emph{instructs}: a comment header lists the
packages the including preamble must load (minus [[inputenc]], which the
includer has already decided on and which is hostile to Xe\LaTeX{}), the
[[\addbibresource]] line, and the need for biber. An abstract cannot be
emitted either, because an [[abstract]] environment belongs to the
including document; it is preserved as comment lines so that text written
by an LLM is not silently lost.

<<review report generation>>=
FRAGMENT_COMMENT_WIDTH = 76


def latex_fragment(
    body: list[str],
    *,
    packages: list[str],
    bib_resource: str | None = None,
    abstract: str | None = None,
    generator: str = "",
) -> list[str]:
    """Wrap `body` in an input-able fragment with an instructional header."""
    lines: list[str] = [r"% --- scholar LaTeX fragment " + "-" * 44]
    if generator:
        lines.append(f"% Generated by: {generator}")
    lines.append(r"% \input this file from the body of a document whose")
    lines.append("% preamble loads:")
    for package in packages:
        if "inputenc" in package:
            continue
        lines.append(f"%   {package}")
    if bib_resource:
        lines.append(f"%   \\addbibresource{{{bib_resource}}}")
        lines.append("% and which is compiled with biber.")
    if r"\begin{refsection}" in body:
        lines.extend(
            [
                "% Citations in this fragment sit in their own refsection,",
                r"% so the including document's \printbibliography is not",
                "% affected. Refsections cannot nest: \\input this file",
                "% outside any refsection of your own.",
            ]
        )
    if abstract:
        lines.append("% Abstract (not emitted; abstracts belong to the")
        lines.append("% including document):")
        for wrapped in textwrap.wrap(abstract, FRAGMENT_COMMENT_WIDTH):
            lines.append(f"%   {wrapped}")
    lines.append("% " + "-" * 70)
    lines.append("")
    lines.extend(body)
    return lines
@

The decision record cites every paper the review touched with
[[\fullcite]], including the discarded ones. In a document that prints a
bibliography, those citations would drag every discarded paper into the
reference list, and a reader could no longer tell which papers the text
actually draws on. biblatex's reference sections solve this cleanly: a
[[refsection]] environment gives its citations their own numbering, and
[[\printbibliography]] defaults to the section it is called in. Wrapping
the decision record therefore keeps the bibliography equal to what the
prose cites, and [[\fullcite]] needs no bibliography to resolve. The same
wrapping protects an including document from a fragment.

<<review report generation>>=
def refsection(lines: list[str]) -> list[str]:
    """Wrap `lines` in a biblatex `refsection` environment."""
    return [r"\begin{refsection}", *lines, r"\end{refsection}"]
@

Let's verify the skeletons: the document has the wrapper and an escaped
title, the fragment has neither the wrapper nor a live
[[\addbibresource]] command, and the header carries the instructions.

<<test functions>>=
class TestLatexSkeleton:
    def test_document_has_skeleton_and_escaped_title(self):
        lines = latex_document(
            [r"\section{Body}"], title="A & B", date="2026-01-01",
            bib_resource="x.bib", abstract="Abs.",
            bibliography=r"\printbibliography",
            appendix=[r"\section{App}"],
        )
        assert lines[0] == r"\documentclass{article}"
        assert r"\addbibresource{x.bib}" in lines
        assert r"\title{A \& B}" in lines
        assert lines[-1] == r"\end{document}"
        assert lines.index(r"\section{Body}") \
            < lines.index(r"\printbibliography") \
            < lines.index(r"\appendix") \
            < lines.index(r"\section{App}")

    def test_fragment_has_no_skeleton(self):
        lines = latex_fragment(
            refsection([r"\section{Body}"]), packages=LATEX_PACKAGES,
            bib_resource="x.bib", abstract="An abstract.",
            generator="scholar test",
        )
        text = "\n".join(lines)
        assert r"\documentclass" not in text
        assert r"\begin{document}" not in text
        assert not any(l.startswith(r"\addbibresource") for l in lines)
        assert r"%   \addbibresource{x.bib}" in lines
        assert "% Generated by: scholar test" in lines
        assert not any("inputenc" in l for l in lines)
        assert any("booktabs" in l for l in lines)
