\subsection{Assembling the report}

The report's abstract is deterministic: it restates the research question
and the counts, which is all a reader needs to decide whether to open the
document.

<<review report generation>>=
def build_review_report_abstract(session: ReviewSession) -> str:
    """Build a deterministic one-paragraph abstract for the review report."""
    kept = len(session.kept_papers())
    discarded = len(session.discarded_papers())
    pending = len(session.pending_papers())
    total = len(session.decisions)

    context = (session.research_context or "").strip()
    if context:
        first = context.splitlines()[0].strip()
        lead = first.rstrip(".") + "."
    else:
        lead = "This report documents a systematic literature review session."

    providers = ", ".join(session.providers)
    summary = (
        f"{lead} The search covered {providers} and yielded {total} papers "
        f"({kept} kept, {discarded} discarded, {pending} pending)."
    )

    if session.snowball_rounds:
        summary += " Manual snowballing was used to discover additional papers."

    return summary
@

The [[.bib]] file is written in both modes: a fragment cannot embed its
bibliography (the [[\addbibresource]] that would read it is preamble-only),
and a standalone document that carries the same sibling file keeps the two
modes interchangeable. The fragment's body sits in a [[refsection]] so an
including document's bibliography is unaffected by the [[\fullcite]]s; the
standalone report has no bibliography command, so it needs no such
protection and its output is unchanged from before fragments existed.

<<review report generation>>=
def generate_latex_report(
    session: ReviewSession,
    output_path: Path,
    *,
    standalone: bool = True,
) -> None:
    """
    Generate a LaTeX report of the review session.

    The report includes the search parameters and research context,
    summary statistics, and the kept and discarded papers grouped by
    theme and motivation. A `.bib` file with the same base name is
    always written. With `standalone` the `.tex` is a compilable
    document; otherwise it is a fragment for `\\input`, whose header
    says what the including document must set up.

    Args:
        session: The ReviewSession to report on.
        output_path: Path for the .tex file (will also create .bib).
        standalone: Emit a compilable document (default) or a fragment.
    """
    decisions = session.kept_papers() + session.discarded_papers()
    keys = report_cite_keys(decisions)

    def cite_key(decision: ReviewDecision) -> str:
        return keys[id(decision)]

    bib_path = output_path.with_suffix(".bib")
    bib_path.write_text(build_report_bibtex(decisions, cite_key))

    body = build_review_provenance_latex(session)
    body.extend(build_review_decisions_latex(session, cite_key))
    abstract = build_review_report_abstract(session)

    if standalone:
        lines = latex_document(
            body,
            title="Literature Review Report",
            date=session.timestamp.strftime("%Y-%m-%d"),
            bib_resource=bib_path.name,
            abstract=abstract,
        )
    else:
        lines = latex_fragment(
            refsection(body),
            packages=LATEX_PACKAGES,
            bib_resource=bib_path.name,
            abstract=abstract,
            generator="scholar sessions export --no-standalone",
        )

    output_path.write_text("\n".join(lines))
@

