\subsection{Audit table}

A backing appendix in a paper must let a reader without access to the
session see every candidate the search returned and why each one was
kept or excluded. The review report is too long for that and is a
document of its own; what the appendix needs is one table. Its rows are
papers, its last column is the reason, and it is always a fragment
because it is meant to be [[\input]] next to the claim it backs.

The reason column encodes the review's own vocabulary. A paper a human
kept is \emph{cited}, under the theme its tags name; the caller may map
theme tags to readable names. The remaining papers were classified, by a
human or by [[scholar llm classify]], into one of four categories that a
two-sided literature search produces: the paper supports the claim,
qualifies or contradicts it, belongs to an adjacent subtopic, or is a
false hit from another field. The order of the categories doubles as the
sort order, so the table reads from the strongest evidence downwards.
Unknown tags and papers still pending sort last.

<<review report generation>>=
AUDIT_CATEGORIES: dict[str, int] = {
